<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Fast DDS profile for the RoboSub stack.

  WHY THIS EXISTS
  ===============
  Symptom: the robot_localization EKF (single-threaded) froze for up to 36s at a
  time when the sub ran UNTETHERED. During a freeze it published nothing
  (/odometry/filtered, /tf, /diagnostics all stopped) so control steered on a
  stale pose while the sub drifted. It escalated over a run (1s -> 4s -> 9s ->
  36s) and logged "Failed to meet update rate! Took 35.8 seconds".

  Root cause: it is NOT power/battery (a separate 400Hz process never missed a
  beat through the freeze). The default RMW is Fast DDS with RELIABLE +
  SYNCHRONOUS writers announcing on ALL interfaces, including the topside tether
  (point-to-point 192.168.2.0/24 on the USB-ethernet adapter). When the tether
  drops, the topside reliable subscriber (rviz / ros2 topic echo / foxglove)
  becomes unreachable; robot_localization calls publish() INSIDE its update loop,
  and a synchronous reliable write() blocks waiting for ACKs that never arrive.
  Single-threaded executor => the whole node freezes until Fast DDS gives up.

  FIX
  ===
  - ASYNCHRONOUS publish mode: write() enqueues and returns immediately; a
    dedicated Fast DDS thread handles delivery + retries. A dead reader or a
    downed interface can no longer block the calling (executor) thread.
  - max_blocking_time = 10ms: belt-and-suspenders cap so even a synchronous path
    can never stall the loop for more than one tick.

  These are the DEFAULT profiles (is_default_profile="true"), so they apply to
  every writer in the stack -- including robot_localization's, whose QoS we can't
  edit from Python. publishMode/max_blocking_time are not ROS 2 QoS fields, so
  the ROS QoS chosen in code is preserved; only the blocking behaviour changes.

  ENABLED VIA: export FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=<repo>/config/fastdds_async.xml
  (wired into launch_sub.sh and run_experiment.sh).
-->
<dds xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
  <profiles>
    <data_writer profile_name="default_async_writer" is_default_profile="true">
      <qos>
        <publishMode>
          <kind>ASYNCHRONOUS</kind>
        </publishMode>
        <reliability>
          <max_blocking_time>
            <sec>0</sec>
            <nanosec>10000000</nanosec>
          </max_blocking_time>
        </reliability>
      </qos>
    </data_writer>
  </profiles>
</dds>
